WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.830 --> 00:00:11.790
Hello everyone. Welcome to the Langfocus Channel and my name is Paul.

00:00:11.790 --> 00:00:18.420
Today's topic is the Polish language, or
język polski as it's called in Polish.

00:00:18.420 --> 00:00:23.330
Polish is the official language of the
Central European country of Poland where

00:00:23.330 --> 00:00:28.140
97% of the population speaks it as a
native language. That makes Poland the

00:00:28.140 --> 00:00:32.820
most linguistically homogeneous country
in Europe. It's the second most widely

00:00:32.820 --> 00:00:37.140
spoken Slavic language after Russian
with around 45 million native speakers

00:00:37.140 --> 00:00:37.640
and an additional 5 million second
language speakers. As you may know, Slavic

00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:45.600
languages are a branch of the
indo-european language family. Between

00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:51.360
the Year 500 CE and 1000 CE, Proto-Slavic gradually diverged into dialects

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:56.520
and by 1000 CE there were already
distinct West Slavic, East Slavic, and

00:00:56.520 --> 00:01:00.840
South Slavic languages. Dialects of
these three languages developed into the

00:01:00.840 --> 00:01:06.030
languages of each Slavic branch. The West
Slavic languages include Polish, Czech

00:01:06.030 --> 00:01:09.600
Slovak, 
Sorbian (a minority language in eastern

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:14.369
Germany) and Kashubian (a minority
language in Poland). Poland first became a

00:01:14.369 --> 00:01:18.360
state in the 10th century around the
time that Slavak was developing into

00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:22.049
various distinct languages. The
unification of various Slavic tribes

00:01:22.049 --> 00:01:26.670
within one Polish state helped tie their
dialects together as a single language.

00:01:26.670 --> 00:01:30.659
The Christianization of Poland took
place in the decades following the

00:01:30.659 --> 00:01:35.100
creation of the Polish state, and along
with that came Latin via the Roman

00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:39.150
Catholic Church, which became the formal
written language and the language of the

00:01:39.150 --> 00:01:42.960
nobility. Polish continued to be the
predominant spoken language among the

00:01:42.960 --> 00:01:47.250
general population, but Latin certainly
had some influence on polish because of

00:01:47.250 --> 00:01:51.270
its prestigious position. Polish was not
a written language until the adoption of

00:01:51.270 --> 00:01:55.590
Christianity and the arrival of Latin.
From that point on vernacular Polish

00:01:55.590 --> 00:01:58.530
began to be written informally using the
Latin alphabet.

00:01:58.530 --> 00:02:03.240
The oldest sample of written Polish that
we know of is a list of names dating

00:02:03.240 --> 00:02:07.560
back to the year 1136. The oldest
written Polish sentence that we know of

00:02:07.560 --> 00:02:11.959
dates back to 1270. 
There are more substantial texts from

00:02:11.959 --> 00:02:16.670
the 14th century onwards as Polish
became more commonly written. From the

00:02:16.670 --> 00:02:19.909
beginning the Latin alphabet was
insufficient for writing all the

00:02:19.909 --> 00:02:24.470
different sounds of Polish, so overtime
Polish writers began using a variety of

00:02:24.470 --> 00:02:28.819
diacritics that have become part of the
Polish alphabet and a characteristic

00:02:28.819 --> 00:02:33.500
part of Polish writing. During the
Jagiellonian dynasty Polish became an

00:02:33.500 --> 00:02:38.209
official language alongside Latin. Also
during this time, cities were founded and

00:02:38.209 --> 00:02:43.280
administered under a German system of
law known as Magdeburg law. Due to the

00:02:43.280 --> 00:02:47.599
influence of this system of law, numerous
German words entered Polish. German

00:02:47.599 --> 00:02:52.790
loanwords in Polish include "ratusz"
meaning "town council", and "bBurmistrz"

00:02:52.790 --> 00:02:57.290
meaning "mayor". Also, there had always been
contact with Germanic speakers,

00:02:57.290 --> 00:03:02.150
especially in the northwestern areas, all
the way back to Proto-Slovak. So there

00:03:02.150 --> 00:03:05.329
are other Germanic loanwords in Polish
that go back a long way.

00:03:05.329 --> 00:03:09.230
Latin remained an official language
alongside Polish through the

00:03:09.230 --> 00:03:13.670
Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth, but
Polish had gradually become more widely

00:03:13.670 --> 00:03:18.049
used by the nobility than Latin. In the
sixteenth century while Polish was

00:03:18.049 --> 00:03:22.370
becoming more standardized, it served as
the vehicle for the Golden Age of Polish

00:03:22.370 --> 00:03:26.299
literature. It also became the lingua
franca throughout much of Central and

00:03:26.299 --> 00:03:30.530
Eastern Europe, spoken by the ruling
classes not only in Lithuania but also

00:03:30.530 --> 00:03:34.760
in Ukraine and Belarus, which were mostly
part of the Commonwealth at the time. The

00:03:34.760 --> 00:03:38.000
third official language of the
Commonwealth was Ruthenian which was

00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:41.569
spoken in the eastern areas and would
later develop into Ukrainian and

00:03:41.569 --> 00:03:45.709
Belarusian dialects. Polish was
influenced by Ruthenian to some extent

00:03:45.709 --> 00:03:52.370
due to contact. Poland was erased from
the map between 795 in 1918 after being

00:03:52.370 --> 00:03:56.750
partitioned between the Russian Empire,
Austria, and the Kingdom of Prussia and

00:03:56.750 --> 00:04:01.459
later the German Empire which it was a
part of. Polish ceased to be an official

00:04:01.459 --> 00:04:04.849
language anywhere and dialectal
differences grew in the different

00:04:04.849 --> 00:04:09.169
occupied parts of Poland. After the First
World War, the borders of Europe were

00:04:09.169 --> 00:04:14.000
redrawn and an independent Polish state
was restored. Lands which were considered

00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:19.260
historically Polish were taken back from
occupying powers, but not all lands.

00:04:19.260 --> 00:04:24.150
The borders were once again redrawn
after World War II with the "Recovered

00:04:24.150 --> 00:04:28.830
Territories" being given from Germany to
Poland, and the Soviet Union occupying

00:04:28.830 --> 00:04:33.960
Eastern areas and making them part of
Belarus and Ukraine. Many Poles were

00:04:33.960 --> 00:04:37.320
forcibly transferred from those
neighboring countries to Poland,

00:04:37.320 --> 00:04:42.390
"transferred" being a euphemism for "kicked
out". This forced transfer of Poles from

00:04:42.390 --> 00:04:46.620
their homes to different dialect areas
resulted in dialect leveling, the loss

00:04:46.620 --> 00:04:52.020
of dialectal differences. And transfer, in
other words expulsion, of non-Poles to

00:04:52.020 --> 00:04:56.460
neighboring countries made Poland a very
linguistically homogeneous country. Today

00:04:56.460 --> 00:05:00.600
four main dialects of Polish are usually
recognized, but the differences are now

00:05:00.600 --> 00:05:06.660
slight. There is Greater Polish; Lesser
Polish; Masovian; and Silesian, though

00:05:06.660 --> 00:05:09.930
speakers often consider themselves a
separate language community; and

00:05:09.930 --> 00:05:14.190
sometimes counted is Kashubian, which is
generally considered a separate language

00:05:14.190 --> 00:05:20.460
but is closely related to Polish. So what
is Polish? Like pronunciation and

00:05:20.460 --> 00:05:24.930
orthography, Polish writing has a number
of characteristic features that are

00:05:24.930 --> 00:05:29.430
quite striking at first glance. The
Polish alphabet has 32 letters, but there

00:05:29.430 --> 00:05:34.560
are also seven digraphs, pairs of letters
which represent individual sounds. Notice

00:05:34.560 --> 00:05:39.180
that 4 of those digraphs feature the
letter Z. This makes Z the most common

00:05:39.180 --> 00:05:42.900
(consonant) letter in Polish writing. And notice that
there are two additional letters that

00:05:42.900 --> 00:05:46.890
are derived from Z which appear in
another two digraphs. If you include

00:05:46.890 --> 00:05:50.310
those, people seeing polish in writing
for the first time willdefinitely

00:05:50.310 --> 00:05:55.620
notice the abundance of Z's and Z-like
letters. But those two Z-derived letters

00:05:55.620 --> 00:06:00.030
are not the only special letters in
Polish. There are 9 in total. This letter (ł)

00:06:00.030 --> 00:06:04.050
is pronounced /w/, while in Polish the letter "w" is normally

00:06:04.050 --> 00:06:08.280
pronounced /v/. These letters represent nasal
vowels

00:06:08.280 --> 00:06:13.290
More specifically, vowels followed by
nasal semivowels. The exact quality of

00:06:13.290 --> 00:06:17.430
the nasal sound varies depending on the
surrounding sounds. And the nasal sound

00:06:17.430 --> 00:06:23.310
is normally absent before ł /w/ or in the
nasal "e" (ę) at the end of a word. For example,

00:06:23.310 --> 00:06:28.080
this word (się) is normally pronounced [ɕɛ]
and is only pronounced like this: [ɕɛ̃w̃]

00:06:28.080 --> 00:06:33.660
in very careful formal speech.
There are some noticeably long consonant

00:06:33.660 --> 00:06:37.440
clusters in Polish. This one has a
cluster of five consonants in a row:

00:06:37.440 --> 00:06:41.729
"bezwzględny" meaning "ruthless". Then
there's this one: przestępstwo

00:06:41.729 --> 00:06:46.319
meaning "crime". It has a
cluster of only four consonants, but

00:06:46.319 --> 00:06:49.949
there's also a digraph in this first
cluster which makes it appear to have

00:06:49.949 --> 00:06:54.060
three consonants instead of two. So to
someone looking at Polish for the first

00:06:54.060 --> 00:06:58.169
time, it probably appears to be full of
long consonant clusters, even though some

00:06:58.169 --> 00:07:02.490
of them are just digraphs. Most simple
Polish consonants are pronounced like

00:07:02.490 --> 00:07:06.990
their English equivalents, but note that
C is pronounced as /ts/ and as I

00:07:06.990 --> 00:07:12.870
mentioned before "w" is pronounced /v/.
And also note that the letters X, V, and Q

00:07:12.870 --> 00:07:18.389
are not used in Polish. The sound /ks/,
usually represented by X in English, is

00:07:18.389 --> 00:07:24.449
spelled KS in Polish. One important
feature of Polish pronunciation is hard

00:07:24.449 --> 00:07:29.400
and soft consonants, a common feature of
Slavic languages. Soft consonants are

00:07:29.400 --> 00:07:33.599
generally palatalized, like a hard
consonant pronounced together with a /j/

00:07:33.599 --> 00:07:37.469
sound.
Aside from "j"  and "l"  which are always

00:07:37.469 --> 00:07:42.479
soft, soft consonants are indicated by an
accent above the letter, or by a "i"

00:07:42.479 --> 00:07:49.979
following the consonant. For example, /s/ - /ɕ/, /n/ - /ɲ/, /p/ - /pʲ/.

00:07:49.979 --> 00:07:54.300
Most of the digraphs we saw before
belong to a subset of hard consonants

00:07:54.300 --> 00:07:58.380
that derived from historically soft
consonants. Why do hard and soft

00:07:58.380 --> 00:08:02.789
consonants matter? Because in Polish some
inflectional suffixes are different

00:08:02.789 --> 00:08:07.520
depending on whether they are preceded
by a hard consonant or a soft consonant.

00:08:07.520 --> 00:08:13.469
Most consonants, specifically obstruents:
stops affricates and fricatives, when

00:08:13.469 --> 00:08:16.889
they appear in consonant clusters, must
all be voiced or voiceless.

00:08:16.889 --> 00:08:21.090
This results in voiced obstruents
becoming voiceless when they come before

00:08:21.090 --> 00:08:27.569
or after a voiceless consonant. For
example, "kwiat" /kfʲat/, łódka /wutka/. This also happens at

00:08:27.569 --> 00:08:32.880
the ends of words. This word meaning "car":
"samochód" /saˈmɔ.xut/, and this word meaning "doctor":

00:08:32.880 --> 00:08:37.169
"lekarz" /ˈlɛ.kaʂ/. This type of devoicing is one
of the main things that keeps Polish

00:08:37.169 --> 00:08:41.159
from being a fully phonetic language, in
other words, a language in which one

00:08:41.159 --> 00:08:44.850
grapheme equals one sound. Another thing
is that the distinction

00:08:44.850 --> 00:08:48.600
between a few sounds has been lost, so
that now these letters are pronounced

00:08:48.600 --> 00:08:56.160
the same. Grammar. Let's start with the
word order of Polish. The unmarked or

00:08:56.160 --> 00:09:01.889
neutral word order in Polish is SVO as
in English. For example, "Moje dziecko jest

00:09:01.889 --> 00:09:05.040
słodkie", meaning my child is cute or my child is

00:09:05.040 --> 00:09:09.810
sweet. The word order here is exactly
like in English: subject-verb-complement.

00:09:09.810 --> 00:09:14.790
The sentence has no object, but the
adjective here is a compliment. Another

00:09:14.790 --> 00:09:19.699
example. "Mężczyzna je obiad", which means
"The man is eating dinner".

00:09:19.699 --> 00:09:24.480
Subject-verb-object. Notice that there is
no definite article in Polish, and

00:09:24.480 --> 00:09:28.649
actually there isn't an indefinite
article either. Now this is the unmarked

00:09:28.649 --> 00:09:32.850
or neutral word order, but polish word
order is quite flexible, and elements of

00:09:32.850 --> 00:09:36.839
the sentence can be moved in order to
emphasize them. In the first sentence we

00:09:36.839 --> 00:09:39.690
could emphasize "my child" by moving it to
the end.

00:09:39.690 --> 00:09:44.399
"Słodkie jest moje dziecko".  In the
second sentence we could emphasize "man"

00:09:44.399 --> 00:09:49.889
by moving it to the end. "Obiad je mężczyzna". So this sentence is now "Dinner - eats -

00:09:49.889 --> 00:09:54.810
the man". Technically, any word order is
possible. though some sound unnatural and

00:09:54.810 --> 00:09:58.649
would be most suitable for written
poetry or something. How can these words

00:09:58.649 --> 00:10:03.089
be moved around freely like this? Doesn't
that get confusing? The main reason is

00:10:03.089 --> 00:10:07.259
that Polish is a highly inflected
language and nouns, among other words,

00:10:07.259 --> 00:10:11.639
have case endings endings that tell us
the function of the word in the sentence.

00:10:11.639 --> 00:10:15.329
Because the function of the word is
obvious from its ending, we don't

00:10:15.329 --> 00:10:19.920
need to use word order or syntax to
determine the function of the word. "Dziecko"

00:10:19.920 --> 00:10:25.290
means "child". This is the singular form
in the nominative case, which is usually

00:10:25.290 --> 00:10:29.459
used for the subject of a sentence or a
predicate adjective after a linking verb.

00:10:29.459 --> 00:10:36.110
There are six other cases in Polish:
genitive, dative, accusative, instrumental,

00:10:36.110 --> 00:10:41.610
locative, and Vocative. These are the
singular forms but there are also plural

00:10:41.610 --> 00:10:46.529
forms. It's important to note that polish
has grammatical gender. "Dziecko" is a

00:10:46.529 --> 00:10:52.019
neuter noun. In their most basic form, the
nominative form, neuter nouns end in ~e, ę,

00:10:52.019 --> 00:10:56.550
~o, or ~um.
There are also masculine and feminine

00:10:56.550 --> 00:11:01.200
nouns. Masculine nouns in their nominative form usually end in a consonant. An

00:11:01.200 --> 00:11:06.540
example is "chłopak" meaning "boy". Some
case endings differ between different

00:11:06.540 --> 00:11:11.970
types of masculine nouns. Compare "chłopak", a masculine person, to "bank", a

00:11:11.970 --> 00:11:16.350
masculine object. Notice there's a
difference in the accusative plural. The

00:11:16.350 --> 00:11:19.710
endings are different for masculine
people on the one hand, and masculine

00:11:19.710 --> 00:11:23.820
objects and animals on the other. For
many nouns this is also true for the

00:11:23.820 --> 00:11:28.110
nominative plural. Also notice that the
genitive and accusative singular endings

00:11:28.110 --> 00:11:32.160
are different. In these cases the endings
are different for masculine animate

00:11:32.160 --> 00:11:36.870
nouns, in other words humans and animals,
on the one hand, and inanimate objects on

00:11:36.870 --> 00:11:41.300
the other. Though in the genitive case a
few types of nouns are exceptions.

00:11:41.300 --> 00:11:46.890
Feminine nouns in their nominative form
usually end in a or i. For example,

00:11:46.890 --> 00:11:52.560
"matka" meaning mother. Adjectives also
agree with nouns in gender, number, and

00:11:52.560 --> 00:11:57.570
case, and they have their own sets of
endings. For example, "młoda matka" means

00:11:57.570 --> 00:12:01.650
"a young mother" in the nominative case.
But here's a phrase in the genitive case:

00:12:01.650 --> 00:12:07.170
"dziecko młodej matki" - the young mothers
child. Not only the noun has a genitive

00:12:07.170 --> 00:12:11.220
case ending, but so does the adjective.
And notice that the adjective's genitive

00:12:11.220 --> 00:12:16.080
ending is different from the noun's.
Adjectives have endings for all cases, in

00:12:16.080 --> 00:12:22.920
all genders, for both singular and plural.
Verbs. Polish verbs have two tenses: past

00:12:22.920 --> 00:12:27.360
and non-past, but there are also two
major aspects which indicate completion

00:12:27.360 --> 00:12:31.950
of an action (the perfective aspect) or
incompletion of an action (the

00:12:31.950 --> 00:12:36.180
imperfective aspect). Each verb has
separate infinitives for the

00:12:36.180 --> 00:12:40.500
imperfective and perfective aspect, and
then conjugations are built around those

00:12:40.500 --> 00:12:44.670
infinitives in order to indicate the
tense (in other words, the time at which

00:12:44.670 --> 00:12:48.690
the complete or incomplete action is
done). Let's take a look at the verb

00:12:48.690 --> 00:12:53.340
meaning "to read". "Czytać" - this is an
imperfective infinitive. In other words,

00:12:53.340 --> 00:12:58.140
the reading is in progress or ongoing.
"Przeczytać". This is a perfective

00:12:58.140 --> 00:13:02.250
infinitive meaning the reading is
complete. The perfective form of verbs is

00:13:02.250 --> 00:13:06.570
derived from the imperfective form by
adding a prefix to it, or sometimes by

00:13:06.570 --> 00:13:11.190
adding a suffix or changing a vowel in
the verb stem now. Let's conjugate these

00:13:11.190 --> 00:13:15.320
in the past tense. You drop the ć
from the end of the infinitive and add

00:13:15.320 --> 00:13:20.660
one of these endings to the remaining
stem. For example, "(Ja) czytałem" means "I

00:13:20.660 --> 00:13:24.500
was reading". In other words, the reading
was ongoing at a certain point in the

00:13:24.500 --> 00:13:27.830
past.
 "(Ja) przeczytałem" means "I read". In other

00:13:27.830 --> 00:13:31.700
words, I completed the action of reading
within a certain time period in the past.

00:13:31.700 --> 00:13:36.020
By the way, please note that Polish is a
"pro-drop" language, meaning that the

00:13:36.020 --> 00:13:40.130
subject pronoun can often be left out.
That's because the verb endings often

00:13:40.130 --> 00:13:44.270
make it clear who the subject is. But
something to note is how the verbs are

00:13:44.270 --> 00:13:49.130
conjugated for gender in the past tense.
In the first and second person singular

00:13:49.130 --> 00:13:53.120
there are masculine and feminine forms.
The third person singular also has a

00:13:53.120 --> 00:13:57.260
neuter form. For the plural forms, the
distinction is between masculine

00:13:57.260 --> 00:14:01.580
personal endings, in other words for
people, and all the others. A masculine

00:14:01.580 --> 00:14:06.020
inanimate object or animal will be
conjugated the same as for feminine or

00:14:06.020 --> 00:14:11.180
neuter nouns. So while a man would say
"(ja) czytałem" a woman would "(ja)

00:14:11.180 --> 00:14:16.490
czytałam". And if you're talking to a man
you would say "Ty czytałeś" but if

00:14:16.490 --> 00:14:20.810
you're talking to a woman you would say
"(Ty) czytałaś". Speakers of other

00:14:20.810 --> 00:14:24.440
Slavic languages won't see anything
strange about this, but speakers of

00:14:24.440 --> 00:14:28.970
Germanic and Romance languages might
need to get used to this. Now let's take

00:14:28.970 --> 00:14:34.610
a look at the non past conjugations. "Non-
past? What do you mean by non-past? Is it

00:14:34.610 --> 00:14:39.320
present or future? Well, that depends on
whether it's imperfective or perfective.

00:14:39.320 --> 00:14:44.390
For an imperfective verb like "czytać",
the non-past conjugation has present

00:14:44.390 --> 00:14:49.370
tense meaning. For a perfective verb like
"przeczytać" the non past conjugation

00:14:49.370 --> 00:14:54.589
has future tense meaning. "(Ona) czyta książkę". This means "She's reading

00:14:54.589 --> 00:14:59.510
a book", while this sentence: on "(Ona) przeczyta książkę" means "She will read a

00:14:59.510 --> 00:15:04.279
book" Unlike the past tense conjugations,
the non-past conjugations don't have any

00:15:04.279 --> 00:15:08.690
gender distinctions. The tricky thing
about the non-past conjugation is that

00:15:08.690 --> 00:15:12.230
there isn't just one of them. There are
actually four different conjugations,

00:15:12.230 --> 00:15:17.270
with virtually all verbs following one
of them. Conjugation I and conjugation

00:15:17.270 --> 00:15:22.490
IV are characterized by an "e" vowel in
the 3rd person singular form. Conjugation

00:15:22.490 --> 00:15:27.230
II by an "i" vowel in the 3rd person
singular form. In conjugation III by

00:15:27.230 --> 00:15:32.960
"a" vowel in the third-person singular. The
perfective non-past form is sometimes

00:15:32.960 --> 00:15:37.130
referred to as the simple future. There's
another compound future tense that

00:15:37.130 --> 00:15:41.390
expresses the imperfective future, in other
words, an action that is ongoing or

00:15:41.390 --> 00:15:45.980
repeating in the future. "(Ona) będzie czytać książkę". This sentence means "She

00:15:45.980 --> 00:15:50.660
will be reading a book". It's formed by
using the future tense of "być" - the word

00:15:50.660 --> 00:15:56.210
meaning "to be", followed by the
imperfective infinitive. "Być" is an

00:15:56.210 --> 00:15:59.540
irregular verb, using a different verb
stem in the past, present, and future

00:15:59.540 --> 00:16:05.030
forms. Here's an example in the present
tense. "Nie jestem Polakiem." This means "I'm not

00:16:05.030 --> 00:16:10.190
Polish". And as a quick side note, here we
see how Polish negation works. You simply

00:16:10.190 --> 00:16:15.230
place the negation particle "nie" before
the verb. Now that we've examined some of

00:16:15.230 --> 00:16:19.070
the major features of Polish, let's take
a look at a few more sentences and see

00:16:19.070 --> 00:16:23.780
what we find. This sentence means "My
father reads 2 books every day". "Mój ojciec

00:16:23.780 --> 00:16:28.430
codziennie czyta dwie książki."
Word-for-word it's: my father - everyday -

00:16:28.430 --> 00:16:33.350
read - two - books. First off we see a
possessive adjectives before the noun

00:16:33.350 --> 00:16:37.760
meaning "father". Possessive adjectives in
Polish are inflected for the gender and

00:16:37.760 --> 00:16:42.440
number of the noun. Here the word "ojciec"
meaning "father" is masculine and

00:16:42.440 --> 00:16:47.600
singular, so the possessive is in it's
masculine singular form. And "ojciec" is

00:16:47.600 --> 00:16:53.660
in the nominative case. Next "codziennie"  is
an adverb meaning "every day". The word for

00:16:53.660 --> 00:16:59.300
"day" is "dzień". Next we see the third-person
masculine singular present tense form of

00:16:59.300 --> 00:17:05.000
the verb. Next we see the number "dwie" 
which means 2. Numbers 1 to 4 in Polish

00:17:05.000 --> 00:17:08.630
have different forms depending on the
case and gender of the noun they are

00:17:08.630 --> 00:17:11.720
referring to.
"Dwie" is feminine and is used for

00:17:11.720 --> 00:17:16.640
nominative and accusative case. Here it's
the accusative. The number is feminine

00:17:16.640 --> 00:17:21.230
because the word for book is feminine.
Książki is the plural form used for

00:17:21.230 --> 00:17:25.820
nominative and accusative cases, and here
it's the accusative. A moment ago I

00:17:25.820 --> 00:17:30.140
mentioned that numbers 1 to 4 have
different numerous forms. Numbers higher

00:17:30.140 --> 00:17:34.760
than 4 have only two forms. When counting
masculine personal nouns they are in the

00:17:34.760 --> 00:17:38.870
genitive case and the nouns they refer
to are in the genitive case. And when

00:17:38.870 --> 00:17:40.970
counting all other nouns they are in the
nominative/

00:17:40.970 --> 00:17:45.890
accusative case, but the noun is still
in the genitive case. So if we change the

00:17:45.890 --> 00:17:49.730
number in this sentence say from two to
five the sentence would look like this.

00:17:49.730 --> 00:17:55.669
"My father reads five books every day".
"Mój ojciec codziennie czyta pięć książek." The

00:17:55.669 --> 00:18:01.340
number five has two forms: pięciu for
masculine people, and pięć for all others,

00:18:01.340 --> 00:18:05.690
including objects like books. "Książek." This noun is in the genitive

00:18:05.690 --> 00:18:10.700
plural form. And one more sentence. This
sentence means "Everyone likes him

00:18:10.700 --> 00:18:15.409
because he often laughs". she's single
"Wszyscy go lubią, bo często  się śmieje." Word-for-

00:18:15.409 --> 00:18:21.620
word it's: everyone-him-they-like-because-
often-reflexive-pronoun-laughs. First we

00:18:21.620 --> 00:18:25.190
see the pronoun meaning "everyone".
Pronouns in Polish have different case

00:18:25.190 --> 00:18:28.789
forms. Since this is the subject of the
sentence, it's in the nominative case.

00:18:28.789 --> 00:18:33.620
Next we see a personal pronoun meaning
"him". Personal pronouns also have

00:18:33.620 --> 00:18:37.429
different case forms. This one is in the
accusative case. There are two other

00:18:37.429 --> 00:18:43.460
accusative forms: jego, niego. "Go" is
used when the pronoun is not being

00:18:43.460 --> 00:18:49.400
emphasized. "Lubią" is the third-person
plural form of the verb "lubić" meaning

00:18:49.400 --> 00:18:53.690
"to like". This verb belongs to conjugation
II and has an "i" vowel in the

00:18:53.690 --> 00:18:58.070
third-person singular.
Next we see "bo", a conjunction meaning

00:18:58.070 --> 00:19:03.620
"because". And after that we see "często" -
an adverb meaning "often". Next we see a

00:19:03.620 --> 00:19:08.750
reflexive verb meaning "he laughs". Polish
has lots of reflexive verbs meaning that

00:19:08.750 --> 00:19:12.770
both the doer and the receiver of the
action are the same. That may not always

00:19:12.770 --> 00:19:16.669
be obvious in terms of meaning, but in
terms of grammar that's how it works.

00:19:16.669 --> 00:19:21.980
This word "się" is a reflexive pronoun
which you simply add to the verb to form

00:19:21.980 --> 00:19:27.049
the reflexive. It has only one form.
Finally something simple in Polish! The

00:19:27.049 --> 00:19:30.350
verb here is in the third-person
singular present tense, and this verb

00:19:30.350 --> 00:19:34.909
belongs to conjugation I. Let me take
this part of the sentence and ask a

00:19:34.909 --> 00:19:39.620
question about it. "Czy wszyscy go lubią?"
meaning "Does everyone like him?" The way

00:19:39.620 --> 00:19:44.030
you ask yes or no questions in Polish is
very simple. You simply add the question

00:19:44.030 --> 00:19:48.830
marker "czy". Let's formulate another
question. This means "Who do they like?"

00:19:48.830 --> 00:19:53.149
"Kogo lubią?" This is an open-ended
question using an interrogative pronoun

00:19:53.149 --> 00:19:56.129
meaning "who". 
Interrogative pronouns in Polish have

00:19:56.129 --> 00:20:00.269
different case forms. This one is in the
accusative case. In the nominative case

00:20:00.269 --> 00:20:04.889
it takes a different form, so if you
wanted to ask "Who likes him?" it would be

00:20:04.889 --> 00:20:11.639
Kto go lubi? "Kto" is the nominative form.
Honorable Mention. This is a last-minute

00:20:11.639 --> 00:20:15.629
addition to the video, but several Polish
speakers have asked me to include this

00:20:15.629 --> 00:20:19.229
particular feature of the language. When
talking about the presence of something

00:20:19.229 --> 00:20:25.440
in Polish you use the verb "być", meaning
"to be". For example, "On tu jest" means "He is

00:20:25.440 --> 00:20:30.239
here". That seems straightforward. The
unusual part is the negative form. "Nie ma

00:20:30.239 --> 00:20:36.299
go tu." NOT- IT- HAS-HIM. So this is like
saying "It doesn't have him here". But who

00:20:36.299 --> 00:20:39.299
is IT?
I don't know, but I suppose it's like a

00:20:39.299 --> 00:20:42.359
dummy subject in English, like when we
say "It's sunny".

00:20:42.359 --> 00:20:47.700
It doesn't really refer to anything. As
you can see Polish is an extremely

00:20:47.700 --> 00:20:51.629
interesting language, but it's also one
that many learners find daunting and

00:20:51.629 --> 00:20:55.409
intimidating. But as with any language,
there are people who learn it, whether

00:20:55.409 --> 00:20:59.099
they move to Poland or they're simply
deeply interested in Polish culture.

00:20:59.099 --> 00:21:02.849
Those people didn't learn everything
about the language all at once. They

00:21:02.849 --> 00:21:06.330
learned certain pieces first, then they
learned other pieces afterwards, until

00:21:06.330 --> 00:21:10.679
eventually they had a fuller picture of
the language. So don't let the details of

00:21:10.679 --> 00:21:14.729
Polish scare you off if you have the
interest and the motivation to go for it.

00:21:14.729 --> 00:21:18.570
The Question of the Day. To native
speakers of Polish: what additional

00:21:18.570 --> 00:21:22.739
features of Polish would you like to
tell us about? For example, are there any

00:21:22.739 --> 00:21:26.669
features of informal speech that you
would like to share? And to students of

00:21:26.669 --> 00:21:30.720
Polish: what do you find most challenging
about the language? Do you have any tips

00:21:30.720 --> 00:21:34.349
on how to deal with it? If you enjoyed
this video then please check out the

00:21:34.349 --> 00:21:38.399
various Langfocus social media
accounts, and of course as always I want

00:21:38.399 --> 00:21:42.389
to give a very special thanks to my
Patreon supporters, especially these ones

00:21:42.389 --> 00:21:46.799
right here on the screen who are my top
tier Patreon supporters, so I want to say

00:21:46.799 --> 00:21:50.729
a very special thanks to them for their
support. And to everyone, thank you for

00:21:50.729 --> 00:22:01.429
watching, and have a nice DAY!

